- Astryx design system (@astryxdesign/*, UI screens inside videos, tools/ui-scenes) — MIT — https://github.com/meta/astryx
  with StyleX (MIT) and React (MIT)
- Playwright / Chromium — Apache-2.0 / BSD-style
- edge-tts — see https://github.com/rany2/edge-tts (output audio is produced by a
  Microsoft online service whose commercial terms are not stated)

Optional engines (installed from Settings → Media)
- Manim (MIT), Blender (GPL — run as a separate program), voice engines listed under
  engine/voice/engines/*/voice.json, each with its own license and commercial-use note shown in the app.

Paid models (video understanding, voice, transcription, embeddings) are reached
only through OpenRouter with a key the user supplies, under OpenRouter's and each model provider's
terms. Free stock footage (Pexels, Pixabay) uses the user's own free keys.

The writer skill (.claude/skills/writer) adapts jtydhr88/screenwriting-skills (MIT, sw-dialogue) and
coreyhaines31/marketingskills (MIT, copywriting, ai-tells, ad-creative hook system). Shot-design rules in the
director skill also draw on zenstory-ai/drama-skills (MIT).

The director skill (.claude/skills/director) (five anchors, scene formula, three-jobs and three-detail rules,
one motivated camera move, rhythm ladder, Murch's rule of six) adapts smixs/visual-skills by Serge Shima (https://github.com/smixs/visual-skills),
licensed CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Changes: translated to Korean, condensed,
and mapped onto Fluma's storyboard fields and lint checks.


The humanizer-ko skill (.claude/skills/humanizer-ko) is DaleSeo/korean-skills `humanizer` (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Dale Seo,
https://github.com/daleseo/korean-skills, commit ae12ba2), renamed only; its license is in that folder. The writer skill
summarizes its Korean AI-style patterns (KatFishNet, arXiv 2503.00032).

Sound effects in engine/assets/sfx are CC0 (public domain): Kenney "Interface Sounds" (kenney.nl), rubberduck "100 CC0 SFX"
and pyranostudios "Air Whoosh" (opengameart.org) — see engine/assets/sfx/CREDITS.txt. Background music is chosen per video
from free-library uploads on YouTube; commercial songs are only suggested for attaching in the Shorts editor at upload.
